 ## Overview
-Isometric Voxel Rendering is an object-order algorithm that renders a voxel model into a fixed isometric projection by iterating over a 2D pixel grid and determining which voxels contribute to each pixel's visible surface [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard perspective voxel renderers by using a strict 2:1 aspect ratio projection where the X, Y, and Z axes are equally foreshortened, and from ray-marching approaches by explicitly calculating the screen-space coordinates of individual voxel faces to draw triangles directly [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Naive Voxel Geometry](11683-naive-voxel-geometry.md) by converting scalar field data into geometric primitives submitted to the graphics pipeline [G].
+Isometric Voxel Rendering is an algorithm that renders a voxel model into an isometric projection by iterating over a 2D pixel grid and determining which voxels contribute to each pixel's visible surface, using a tiling system to draw triangles for exposed faces [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Naive Voxel Geometry](11683-naive-voxel-geometry.md) by converting scalar field data into geometric primitives submitted to the graphics pipeline [G].
 
